# Implementation Plan: pme-lattice/inversion.rs

## Module Purpose
First Principle: Avoid Stupidity Framework
- Defines cognitive biases and systematic methods to avoid stupid decisions in product design
- Implements inversion thinking (think backward from failure to success)

## Types to Implement

### 1. CognitiveBias (Enum)
Common cognitive biases in product decisions:
- ConfirmationBias (seeking info that confirms beliefs)
- SurvivorshipBias (focusing on successes, ignoring failures)
- SunkCostFallacy (continuing due to past investment)
- AvailabilityHeuristic (overweighting recent/vivid info)
- AnchoringBias (over-relying on first information)
- OptimismBias (underestimating risks)
- BandwagonEffect (following the crowd)
- DunningKruger (overestimating competence)

### 2. InversionQuestion (Struct)
Questions to think backward from failure:
- id: Uuid
- category: InversionCategory
- question: String
- negative_scenario: String (what failure looks like)
- prevention_strategy: Option<String>

### 3. InversionCategory (Enum)
Categories for inversion questions:
- MarketFailure
- ProductFailure
- TeamFailure
- ExecutionFailure
- CompetitionFailure

### 4. StupidityCheck (Struct)
Checklist to avoid stupid decisions:
- id: Uuid
- bias_type: CognitiveBias
- check_question: String
- passed: Option<bool>
- evidence: Option<String>

### 5. InversionAnalysis (Struct)
Result of running inversion analysis:
- id: Uuid
- scenario_id: Option<Uuid>
- biases_detected: Vec<CognitiveBias>
- checks: Vec<StupidityCheck>
- inversion_questions: Vec<InversionQuestion>
- failure_modes_identified: Vec<String>
- prevention_strategies: Vec<String>
- risk_score: f32
- created_at: DateTime<Utc>

### 6. InversionError (Enum)
Error types for inversion module

